    "far_field_metric": {
      "metric": "ds^2 = -dt^2 + dr^2 + (r^2+tau^2) dOmega^2 exactly, for all r (MRV eq. ABJ). g_tt = -1 exactly: no 1/r term at any radius.",
      "ADM_mass": 0.0,
      "J_asymptotics": "J = |r| + tau^2/(2|r|) + O(r^-3); areal-radius correction only, no Newtonian potential.",
      "curvature_falloff": "Einstein-tensor (effective) components ~ tau^2/(r^2+tau^2)^2 ~ tau^2/r^4 (Phase 3, exact)."
    },
    "scalar": {
      "pi_prime_at_1e4": 0.000999999500000375,
      "pi_prime_law": "pi' = tau/J = tau/sqrt(r^2+tau^2) ~ tau/r (power fit -1); pi itself = tau*arcsinh(r/tau) ~ tau*ln(2|r|/tau) grows logarithmically and never settles to a constant.",
      "long_range_force_paragraph": "No on-background long-range scalar force on a test particle arises at linear order. The published action contains no matter sector at all (L_matter = 0 in the example), so a test particle couples only to the metric; the background metric has A = B = 1 exactly, hence Gamma^r_tt = 0 and a static test particle is geodesic at every r -- there is no Newtonian 1/r force from the throat (ADM = 0) and no direct pi-mediated force, because no pi-matter coupling is specified. The scalar gradient pi' ~ tau/r is long-range as a field profile, and scalar perturbations do mix with metric perturbations (that mixing is the even sector audited in Phase 3 / F4-1..3), but on this background it produces no additional static force on a minimally coupled test body. Honest limit: this is an on-background statement only. A real star or binary would source pi through the (off-trajectory) field equations, and per Q3 the published construction fixes no unique prediction for that situation."
